import redox_ast::{Binding, Ident, Pattern}

-- | The one name a binding contributes to `RDX1003`, if it contributes one.
-- |
-- | Plan 54 leaf 3. `Binding::pattern` is a [`Pattern`] and not an [`Ident`] —
-- | plan 33 made `(a, b) = point` a binding rather than a second statement
-- | kind — so a lint over bindings has to decide what a destructuring one is
-- | worth.
-- |
-- | # A destructuring binding contributes **nothing**
-- |
-- | Only `Pattern::Binding`, a bare name. `(a, b) = point`,
-- | `[first, .., last] = xs`, `Point { x, y } = p` and `whole @ Move x y = m`
-- | all answer `None`, however many names they bind.
-- |
-- | The reason is the **suggestion**, which is all this lint has to offer. It
-- | says *write `:=` instead*, and `:=` assigns to every place on its left —
-- | so the rewrite is only sound when the statement rebinds *exactly* the
-- | names already bound. A destructuring binding that overlaps an earlier one
-- | on **some** of its names is the ordinary case, and there the suggestion is
-- | a program that does not compile: `(a, c) := other` after `(a, b) = point`
-- | assigns to a `c` nothing ever bound, which is rustc's `E0425`. A warning
-- | whose advice breaks the build is worse than no warning.
-- |
-- | # What the other answer would have cost
-- |
-- | Collecting every name a pattern binds — recursing through `Tuple`,
-- | `Slice`, `Record`, `Apply`, `Or`, and taking `As::name` and
-- | `NamedRest::name` — would catch two destructurings of one shape, which
-- | `:=` *can* express. It costs the recursion itself, the unsound suggestion
-- | above on the partial-overlap case, and a judgement about `Pattern::Or`
-- | that has no good answer: an alternation binds a name on *some* branch, so
-- | "already bound" stops being a yes-or-no question.
-- |
-- | The narrower rule is wrong in one direction only — it stays silent about a
-- | shadow that is real — and that direction is already where this lint lives:
-- | plan 54 §6 discloses that `=` goes on shadowing across block boundaries
-- | too. Silence is this lint's documented residue; bad advice is not.
-- |
-- | A literal, a wildcard, a range or a nullary-looking constructor pattern
-- | binds nothing in the first place, so `None` is simply true for them.
export(crate) binding_name : &Binding -> Option<&Ident>
binding_name binding = case &binding.pattern of
  Pattern::Binding ident -> Some ident
  Pattern::Literal { .. } or Pattern::Wildcard _ or Pattern::Range { .. } or Pattern::Apply { .. } or Pattern::Tuple { .. } or Pattern::Slice { .. } or Pattern::Record { .. } or Pattern::As { .. } or Pattern::Rest _ or Pattern::NamedRest { .. } or Pattern::Or _ -> None
